# Using ArchiPy Services

Services are thin transport adapters (HTTP routers / gRPC servicers). Bootstrap often lives in `manage.py` — follow this rule there too.

Live: https://syntaxarc.github.io/ArchiPy/getting-started/project_structure/

## Responsibilities

- Validate and translate HTTP/gRPC requests into domain `*InputDTO`.
- Call the appropriate logic class; return domain `*OutputDTO`.
- Let AppUtils exception handlers/interceptors map ArchiPy `BaseError` status metadata. Add transport-specific mapping
  only when the domain error has no suitable mapping; never expose internal exception text.
- Version in path: `services/{domain}/v{n}/` — not in business logic.

## REST resource paths (FastAPI)

Paths are hierarchical resources: `/api/v{n}/<collection>/{<resource>_uuid}/<child-collection>/{<child>_uuid}/...`

```text
/api/v1/users/{user_uuid}/campaigns/{campaign_uuid}/ad-groups/{ad_group_uuid}/ad-items/{ad_item_uuid}
```

- **kebab-case** for every literal segment (`ad-groups`, `ad-items`) — never `snake_case`, `camelCase`, or `PascalCase`.
- Plural nouns for collections; the HTTP method is the verb, so no `/get-user` or `/createCampaign`. Use a sub-resource
  or a kebab-case action segment only when no CRUD verb fits (`POST .../campaigns/{campaign_uuid}/pause`).
- Nesting expresses ownership: a child id is only meaningful under its parent, and the logic must verify the parent
  owns the child instead of trusting the URL.
- Path parameters are `snake_case` Python names typed `UUID` (`user_uuid: UUID`), so FastAPI validates them; keep the
  placeholder name identical at every level (`{campaign_uuid}`, not `{id}`).
- Version prefix `/api/v{n}` comes from the router (`APIRouter(prefix="/api/v1")`), one version per
  `services/{domain}/v{n}/`. Do not repeat the version in child routes.
- Collection root has no trailing slash (`/api/v1/users`); do not mix trailing-slash styles.

## Actor identity and audit columns (JWT auth)

With any JWT-based auth (`KeycloakUtils` dependencies/decorators, `JWTUtils`, or another issuer) the acting principal comes
from the verified access token, never from a path parameter. With `KeycloakUtils` it is an `AuthenticatedUserDTO`; for
other JWTs read the claims the same way: actor = `act.sub` (RFC 8693 delegation/impersonation) when present, else `sub`.

- Fill `created_by*` / `updated_by*` (`created_by_admin*`, `updated_by_admin*` on `AdminEntity` /
  `UpdatableAdminEntity`) **first** from the actor (`auth.current_actor.id`, or the `act` claim: the impersonating
  admin/client), and **only if there is no actor** from the access-token subject (`auth.user_id`, or `sub`).
- Never take it from the URL: a path owner uuid (`{user_uuid}`, `{customer_uuid}`, `{merchant_uuid}`, `{admin_uuid}`, or any
  other principal type) identifies the resource owner (the target of the request), not the actor. It may differ from the
  caller (admin acting on a customer, impersonation).
- Resolve it once in the service layer (`actor_uuid = UUID(auth.current_actor.id if auth.current_actor else auth.user_id)`),
  pass it as an explicit `actor_uuid` field on the `*InputDTO` / `*CommandDTO`; logics and repositories never read the
  token or request.
- Admin entities take the actor uuid in `created_by_admin_uuid`; manager entities in `created_by_uuid`
  (and `updated_by_*` on every update).

```python
@router.patch("/merchants/{merchant_uuid}/campaigns/{campaign_uuid}")
def update_campaign(merchant_uuid: UUID, campaign_uuid: UUID, body: UpdateCampaignBody,
                    auth: AuthenticatedUserDTO = Depends(<jwt_auth_dependency>)) -> CampaignOutputDTO:
    actor_uuid = UUID(auth.current_actor.id if auth.current_actor else auth.user_id)  # not merchant_uuid
    return logic.update(UpdateCampaignInputDTO(owner_uuid=merchant_uuid, campaign_uuid=campaign_uuid, actor_uuid=actor_uuid, ...))
```

## Bootstrap via AppUtils + FastAPIConfig

```bash
uv add "archipy[fastapi]"   # and/or archipy[grpc]
```

```python
from archipy.helpers.utils.app_utils import AppUtils
from archipy.configs.base_config import BaseConfig

# FastAPI — reads BaseConfig.FASTAPI for title, docs, middleware
app = AppUtils.create_fastapi_app()
app.include_router(create_<domain>_v1_router(container))

# gRPC sync / async — do not mix servicer styles
config = BaseConfig.global_config()
server = AppUtils.create_grpc_app(config)           # sync servicers
# server = AppUtils.create_async_grpc_app(config)    # async servicers
```

Do not re-implement CORS, exception handlers, or stock gRPC interceptors that AppUtils already wires.

Each domain exports its transport wiring so the entrypoint and the BDD harness share it:
`create_<domain>_v{n}_router(container)` for FastAPI and `register_<domain>_v{n}_servicers(server, container)` for
gRPC (adds servicers to an `AppUtils`-built server; never binds a port).

## manage.py / uvicorn

Bind from `config.FASTAPI` — never hardcode host/port/reload/proxy:

```python
config = BaseConfig.global_config()
uvicorn.run(
    "manage:create_app",
    factory=True,
    host=config.FASTAPI.SERVE_HOST,
    port=config.FASTAPI.SERVE_PORT,
    reload=config.FASTAPI.RELOAD,
    proxy_headers=config.FASTAPI.PROXY_HEADERS,
    forwarded_allow_ips=config.FASTAPI.FORWARDED_ALLOW_IPS or "127.0.0.1",
)
```

CLI flags may override for local runs; defaults always come from `FASTAPI`.

## Do Not

- Put business rules or `@postgres_sqlalchemy_atomic_decorator` in services.
- Hand-roll bare `FastAPI()` / `grpc.server()` when AppUtils extras are installed.
- Hardcode uvicorn host/port — use `FastAPIConfig`.
- Use non-kebab literal path segments, verb paths (`/get-user`), or flat routes that hide the resource hierarchy.
- Catch every domain error in each route/servicer when AppUtils already provides centralized mapping.
